---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content'
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { KINDS, KIND_NOTES, TOPICS, GEOGRAPHIES, keysOf } from '../taxonomy'

const all = await getCollection('entries')
const shown = all.filter((e) => e.data.status !== 'dead')

const kinds = keysOf(KINDS).map((k) => ({
  value: k,
  label: KINDS[k],
  note: KIND_NOTES[k],
  count: shown.filter((e) => e.data.kind === k).length,
}))

const listed = [
  'Organisations doing civic, research or advocacy work',
  'Open datasets, and the portals that hold them',
  'Tools you can run without buying anything',
  'Reporting and research that shows its sources',
  'Archives, including of things that no longer exist',
  'Communities you can actually join and ask questions in',
]

const notListed = [
  'Consultancies and vendors selling into the sector',
  'Products whose free tier is really a trial',
  'Anything primarily promotional about itself',
  'Work you cannot reach without knowing someone',
  'Individual reports — we list who published them, not each PDF',
]

const counts = {
  topics: Object.keys(TOPICS).length,
  places: Object.keys(GEOGRAPHIES).length,
}
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="What this index is, what gets listed, and how it is kept honest."
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p class="statement">
      Good civic work is badly indexed. The organisation that already built
      the dataset you need, the tool that would have saved you three weeks,
      the archive holding records you assumed were lost — these exist, and
      finding them mostly depends on <em>knowing someone who already
      knows</em>. This is an attempt to write that knowledge down.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="about-block">
    <h2>The name</h2>
    <div class="prose-col">
      <p>
        <em>Soochi</em> comes from the Kannada <a
          href="https://alar.ink/dictionary/kannada/english/%E0%B2%B8%E0%B3%82%E0%B2%9A%E0%B2%BF"
          lang="kn" hreflang="kn" rel="noopener">ಸೂಚಿ</a> (<i>sūci</i>): among its meanings is an index of a book's chapters or subjects.
        That is the job here too — a small, ordered way into a much larger body of work.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="about-block">
    <h2>The seven kinds</h2>
    <p class="lead">
      Every entry is exactly one of these. The vocabulary is deliberately
      small — an index where each entry invents its own tags is a list, not
      an index.
    </p>

    <ul class="kind-table">
      {kinds.map((k) => (
        <li data-kind={k.value}>
          <span class="dot" aria-hidden="true" />
          <span class="k-name">{k.label}</span>
          <span class="k-note">{k.note}</span>
          <span class="k-count">{k.count}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <p class="footnote">
      Alongside these, {counts.topics} topics and {counts.places} places, both
      closed lists. Adding a term is a deliberate edit someone reviews, which
      is most of what keeps the filters meaning anything.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="about-block">
    <h2>What gets listed</h2>
    <div class="two-up">
      <div>
        <h3 class="col-head col-head--in">In</h3>
        <ul class="marks marks--in">
          {listed.map((x) => <li>{x}</li>)}
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="col-head col-head--out">Out</h3>
        <ul class="marks marks--out">
          {notListed.map((x) => <li>{x}</li>)}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="footnote">
      Paywalled work is listed when the work itself is the point, and
      labelled as such. The bar is usefulness, not size or reputation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="about-block">
    <h2>How it stays honest</h2>
    <div class="two-up">
      <div class="prose-col">
        <p>
          Every link is checked automatically each week. Entries that go dark
          are marked <em>dormant</em>, then <em>dead</em>, rather than deleted
          — what an organisation used to be is often worth knowing, and a
          directory that quietly drops its failures is lying about its own
          history.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="prose-col">
        <p>
          Descriptions are hand-written and capped at 160 characters. Nothing
          here is scraped. That cap is doing real work: it is the difference
          between a page you can scan and a page of marketing copy someone
          else wrote.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="about-block">
    <h2>The data is the point</h2>
    <div class="prose-col">
      <p>
        Every entry is a plain text file in a public Git repository,
        validated against a schema. The whole index is available as
        <a href="/index.json">index.json</a> under CC0 — take it, fork it,
        build something better with it. Nothing here is locked in a database
        that only one person can reach, and if this site disappears the
        index itself does not.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="about-block about-cta">
    <h2>Who maintains it</h2>
    <div class="prose-col">
      <p>
        One person, with help. The index is only as good as the number of
        people putting things into it — if something is wrong, out of date,
        or missing, <a href="/contribute">say so</a>. Corrections are as
        welcome as additions, and get merged faster.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
